WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:08.192
NARRATOR: Begin by framing Course Foundations
& Scope as a question about inputs, decisions,

2
00:00:08.192 --> 00:00:09.937
and observable results.

3
00:00:09.937 --> 00:00:15.625
NARRATOR: This accelerated course covers
material from the first-year sequence of

4
00:00:15.625 --> 00:00:21.312
courses and provides the foundation for all
subsequent Computer Science courses.

5
00:00:21.312 --> 00:00:27.984
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: Students
will use this course to prepare for advanced

6
00:00:27.984 --> 00:00:31.796
topics like algorithms, data structures, and
software engineering.

7
00:00:31.796 --> 00:00:39.971
NARRATOR: On screen, display a course roadmap
graphic showing CSCI-140 as the central hub

8
00:00:39.971 --> 00:00:42.890
connecting to advanced CS courses.

9
00:00:42.890 --> 00:00:48.832
NARRATOR: List the givens before choosing a
method, because an unstated assumption can

10
00:00:48.832 --> 00:00:50.203
change the conclusion.

11
00:00:50.203 --> 00:00:56.062
NARRATOR: This establishes the vocabulary and
scope needed for the remaining sections.

12
00:00:56.186 --> 00:01:02.874
NARRATOR: Now define Static vs. Dynamic Data
Types precisely enough that another learner

13
00:01:02.874 --> 00:01:04.932
could test the definition.

14
00:01:04.932 --> 00:01:12.215
NARRATOR: Topics include tree and graph
structures, nested data structures, objects,

15
00:01:12.215 --> 00:01:17.511
classes, inheritance, interfaces,
object-oriented collection class libraries for

16
00:01:17.511 --> 00:01:26.118
abstract data types (for example stacks,
queues, maps, and trees), and static versus

17
00:01:26.118 --> 00:01:28.104
dynamic data types.

18
00:01:28.104 --> 00:01:34.307
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: In
statically typed languages, variable types are

19
00:01:34.307 --> 00:01:40.510
checked at compile time, whereas dynamically
typed languages check types at runtime.

20
00:01:40.510 --> 00:01:48.026
NARRATOR: On screen, show a side-by-side
comparison table of static vs. dynamic typing

21
00:01:48.026 --> 00:01:53.229
with code snippets highlighting compile-time
vs. runtime type resolution.

22
00:01:53.229 --> 00:01:59.909
NARRATOR: Separate the formal definition from
an implementation detail, then identify what

23
00:01:59.909 --> 00:02:02.135
evidence would distinguish them.

24
00:02:02.135 --> 00:02:08.807
NARRATOR: A precise definition makes later
examples easier to evaluate and revise.

25
00:02:08.930 --> 00:02:15.083
NARRATOR: With the foundation in place,
examine how Object-Oriented Design Foundations

26
00:02:15.083 --> 00:02:17.880
operates in a concrete sequence.

27
00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:24.044
NARRATOR: Software qualities related to object
orientation, namely cohesion, minimal

28
00:02:24.044 --> 00:02:32.057
coupling, modifiability, and extensibility,
are all introduced in this course, as well as

29
00:02:32.057 --> 00:02:35.755
a few elementary object-oriented design
patterns.

30
00:02:35.755 --> 00:02:42.787
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: A parent
class defines shared behavior, while child

31
00:02:42.787 --> 00:02:47.114
classes inherit and extend that behavior
through polymorphism.

32
00:02:47.114 --> 00:02:53.150
NARRATOR: On screen, render a class diagram
illustrating inheritance relationships between

33
00:02:53.150 --> 00:02:56.442
a base class and derived classes.

34
00:02:56.442 --> 00:03:03.270
NARRATOR: Follow every arrow in order and name
the state before and after each transition.

35
00:03:03.270 --> 00:03:08.942
NARRATOR: The trace converts an abstract
statement into an inspectable sequence.

36
00:03:09.050 --> 00:03:15.366
NARRATOR: Next, compare the choices involved
in Abstract Data Types & Collections and make

37
00:03:15.366 --> 00:03:17.171
the decision criteria explicit.

38
00:03:17.171 --> 00:03:24.746
NARRATOR: The Python language reference
specifies lexical analysis, data model

39
00:03:24.746 --> 00:03:31.563
behavior, execution, expressions, statements,
functions, classes, exceptions, and module

40
00:03:31.563 --> 00:03:33.078
import semantics.

41
00:03:33.078 --> 00:03:40.222
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: A stack
implements last-in-first-out behavior, while a

42
00:03:40.222 --> 00:03:44.984
queue implements first-in-first-out behavior
for ordered data processing.

43
00:03:44.984 --> 00:03:53.199
NARRATOR: On screen, animate a stack and queue
data structure showing push/pop and

44
00:03:53.199 --> 00:03:56.359
enqueue/dequeue operations with labeled
elements.

45
00:03:56.359 --> 00:04:03.459
NARRATOR: Compare alternatives against the
same constraints so the evaluation does not

46
00:04:03.459 --> 00:04:05.234
move the goalposts.

47
00:04:05.234 --> 00:04:12.156
NARRATOR: Using shared criteria turns a
preference into a defensible technical choice.

48
00:04:12.266 --> 00:04:19.200
NARRATOR: Apply Software Qualities: Cohesion &
Minimal Coupling to a worked situation instead

49
00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:22.934
of treating it as an isolated definition.

50
00:04:22.934 --> 00:04:28.137
NARRATOR: Concepts of object- oriented design
are a large part of the course.

51
00:04:28.137 --> 00:04:35.644
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: High
cohesion means a class performs a single,

52
00:04:35.644 --> 00:04:40.841
well-defined purpose, while minimal coupling
reduces dependencies between classes.

53
00:04:40.841 --> 00:04:46.690
NARRATOR: On screen, display a
before-and-after class diagram showing how

54
00:04:46.690 --> 00:04:50.200
refactoring improves cohesion and reduces
coupling.

55
00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:56.569
NARRATOR: Work from the concrete inputs to the
result, checking units, types, or invariants

56
00:04:56.569 --> 00:04:57.934
at each step.

57
00:04:57.934 --> 00:05:02.762
NARRATOR: The worked case shows both how to
apply the method and how to check it.

58
00:05:02.882 --> 00:05:08.984
NARRATOR: Examine the tradeoffs in Exception
Handling & Control Flow, including what

59
00:05:08.984 --> 00:05:12.035
improves and what becomes more difficult.

60
00:05:12.035 --> 00:05:18.373
NARRATOR: Exceptions interrupt normal control
flow until a matching handler processes them

61
00:05:18.373 --> 00:05:19.957
or they propagate.

62
00:05:19.957 --> 00:05:26.102
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: A
try-catch block catches a runtime error,

63
00:05:26.102 --> 00:05:31.222
allowing the program to recover or exit
gracefully without crashing.

64
00:05:31.222 --> 00:05:37.349
NARRATOR: On screen, show a control flow
diagram with a normal path and an exception

65
00:05:37.349 --> 00:05:39.800
path branching to a handler block.

66
00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:46.159
NARRATOR: State the benefit, the cost, and the
condition under which the tradeoff is

67
00:05:46.159 --> 00:05:46.613
acceptable.

68
00:05:46.613 --> 00:05:54.050
NARRATOR: Making the tradeoff visible prevents
a local improvement from hiding a larger cost.

69
00:05:54.170 --> 00:06:01.485
NARRATOR: Stress-test the reasoning around
Compiler & IR Concepts with an edge case and

70
00:06:01.485 --> 00:06:04.620
identify which assumption controls the
outcome.

71
00:06:04.620 --> 00:06:12.290
NARRATOR: Compiler work separates parsing and
semantic analysis from lowering, verification,

72
00:06:12.290 --> 00:06:15.776
optimization, and target-specific code
generation.

73
00:06:15.776 --> 00:06:24.220
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: LLVM IR
serves as a typed, SSA-based intermediate

74
00:06:24.220 --> 00:06:29.416
representation that enables cross-platform
optimization and code generation.

75
00:06:29.416 --> 00:06:37.857
NARRATOR: On screen, illustrate a compiler
pipeline diagram with stages: parsing,

76
00:06:37.857 --> 00:06:43.229
semantic analysis, lowering, verification,
optimization, code generation.

77
00:06:43.229 --> 00:06:49.243
NARRATOR: Change one assumption at a time and
observe which part of the reasoning no longer

78
00:06:49.243 --> 00:06:49.619
holds.

79
00:06:49.619 --> 00:06:55.758
NARRATOR: The edge case reveals the boundary
of the claim without discarding the useful

80
00:06:55.758 --> 00:06:56.635
core idea.

81
00:06:56.738 --> 00:07:04.082
NARRATOR: Use Python Language Model &
Execution to diagnose a failure by separating

82
00:07:04.082 --> 00:07:06.906
symptoms, causes, and corrective action.

83
00:07:06.906 --> 00:07:14.729
NARRATOR: Programs should be reasoned about
through explicit state, control flow, data

84
00:07:14.729 --> 00:07:18.641
representation, contracts, tests, and
observable behavior.

85
00:07:18.641 --> 00:07:25.766
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: Debugging
separates syntax, runtime, and logical

86
00:07:25.766 --> 00:07:32.891
failures, while decomposition and abstraction
reduce interacting concerns in Python code.

87
00:07:32.891 --> 00:07:41.236
NARRATOR: On screen, show a Python object
model diagram with identity, type, and value

88
00:07:41.236 --> 00:07:44.812
attributes, alongside a debugging workflow
flowchart.

89
00:07:44.812 --> 00:07:50.446
NARRATOR: Locate the first point where
observed behavior diverges from expected

90
00:07:50.446 --> 00:07:54.031
behavior, then test the smallest plausible
cause.

91
00:07:54.031 --> 00:08:00.206
NARRATOR: The diagnosis is strongest when
another person can reproduce the observation

92
00:08:00.206 --> 00:08:01.750
and the test.

93
00:08:01.874 --> 00:08:07.132
NARRATOR: Connect Object-Oriented Design
Patterns to the earlier ideas and explain how

94
00:08:07.132 --> 00:08:09.761
the pieces work as one system.

95
00:08:09.761 --> 00:08:16.161
NARRATOR: The course stresses problem solving
while covering modern software development

96
00:08:16.161 --> 00:08:19.652
techniques and introducing essential software
tools.

97
00:08:19.652 --> 00:08:27.037
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: The
singleton pattern ensures a class has only one

98
00:08:27.037 --> 00:08:32.839
instance, while the factory pattern creates
objects without specifying exact classes.

99
00:08:32.839 --> 00:08:40.196
NARRATOR: On screen, display a simple UML
diagram of a factory pattern with creator,

100
00:08:40.196 --> 00:08:42.823
product, and concrete product classes.

101
00:08:42.823 --> 00:08:51.523
NARRATOR: Explain each connection explicitly
and verify that information retains the same

102
00:08:51.523 --> 00:08:53.698
meaning across representations.

103
00:08:53.698 --> 00:09:00.648
NARRATOR: The synthesis matters only if every
connection remains supported by the original

104
00:09:00.648 --> 00:09:01.183
evidence.

105
00:09:01.298 --> 00:09:07.178
NARRATOR: Finish with Practical Analysis
Checklist as a practical checklist that can

106
00:09:07.178 --> 00:09:09.138
guide a new analysis.

107
00:09:09.138 --> 00:09:14.560
NARRATOR: Valid IR must satisfy structural and
typing rules.

108
00:09:14.560 --> 00:09:21.881
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: A
checklist evaluates whether each class has a

109
00:09:21.881 --> 00:09:27.513
single responsibility, limited dependencies,
and clear extension points for future

110
00:09:27.513 --> 00:09:28.076
features.

111
00:09:28.076 --> 00:09:35.122
NARRATOR: On screen, show a printable
checklist template with checkboxes for

112
00:09:35.122 --> 00:09:38.966
cohesion, coupling, modifiability, and
extensibility evaluation.

113
00:09:38.966 --> 00:09:46.431
NARRATOR: For each checklist item, record the
evidence, the remaining uncertainty, and the

114
00:09:46.431 --> 00:09:48.154
